const HAST=[
{id:'pnomoni',n:'Lobar pneumonia (community-acquired)',tag:'Acute · consolidation',
 ozet:'Alveoli fill with inflammatory exudate. Example: right lower-lobe consolidation; the commonest organism is Streptococcus pneumoniae.',
 why:['The organism reaches the lower airway by aspiration (commonest), inhalation or the bloodstream','Loss of defences: smoking, alcohol (aspiration), COPD, after a viral infection, diabetes, old age, splenectomy (encapsulated bacteria)','In the community: S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella), respiratory viruses'],
 how:'Bacteria multiply in the alveolus; alveolar macrophages release IL-1, TNF-α and IL-8, neutrophils migrate in and capillary permeability rises. Alveoli fill with fluid, fibrin, neutrophils and red cells (consolidation) and the process spreads through the lobe via the pores of Kohn. Classic stages: congestion → red hepatisation → grey hepatisation → resolution. The consolidated area is not ventilated but is still perfused: a right-to-left shunt (V/Q = 0) → hypoxaemia that responds poorly to oxygen. Systemic cytokines cause fever, leucocytosis and an acute-phase response (CRP, procalcitonin).',
 bul:['Sudden fever, rigors, cough with purulent (rusty) sputum','Pleuritic chest pain, dyspnoea, tachypnoea, tachycardia','The elderly may present only with confusion and falls; fever may be absent'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Tachypnoea; reduced chest movement on the affected side'],['Palpation','Increased tactile fremitus (solid tissue conducts vibration well)'],['Percussion','Dull'],['Auscultation','Bronchial breath sounds, inspiratory crackles; egophony (“ee” heard as “ay”), bronchophony and whispered pectoriloquy. A friction rub if the pleura is involved.']],
 xrt:['Lobar or segmental homogeneous opacity with air bronchograms (air-filled bronchi surrounded by consolidated alveoli)','Silhouette sign: the right lower lobe blurs the diaphragm, the middle lobe the right heart border, the lingula the left heart border','Lobar volume is preserved without fissural displacement (unlike collapse)','Radiological resolution may lag clinical recovery by 6–12 weeks: in smokers over 50, repeat the film after 6–8 weeks (underlying tumour)'],
 tani:[['Clinical + film','Diagnosis = a new infiltrate + a compatible clinical picture. Lung ultrasound (consolidation, B-lines) helps at the bedside.'],
       ['Severity','CURB-65 or PSI; for intensive care, the IDSA/ATS major criteria (mechanical ventilation, septic shock needing vasopressors).'],
       ['Laboratory','Leucocytosis, raised CRP and procalcitonin; urea, electrolytes (hyponatraemia in Legionella), liver tests, oxygen saturation ± arterial blood gas.'],
       ['Organism','In admitted patients: sputum Gram stain and culture, two sets of blood cultures, urinary pneumococcal and Legionella antigens, influenza/SARS-CoV-2 PCR in season. Not routinely needed in mild outpatients.'],
       ['Arterial blood gas','Hypoxaemia with respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation; a rising PaCO₂ as the patient tires is a bad sign. Raised A–a gradient.']],
 etki:[['kan','Sepsis and septic shock','Pneumonia is the commonest source of sepsis: vasodilatation, hypotension, rising lactate, multi-organ failure.',null],
       ['plevra','Parapneumonic effusion and empyema','About 40% develop an effusion; pH <7.2 or frank pus needs a chest drain.','#akciger/hastalik/efuzyon'],
       ['kalp','Heart: arrhythmia, ischaemia, heart failure','About 20–30% of admitted pneumonia patients have a major cardiac event: new AF, type 2 MI, decompensated heart failure. Pneumococcus can also injure the myocardium directly.','#durumlar/af'],
       ['kalp','Endocarditis and pericarditis','Pneumococcal bacteraemia rarely causes endocarditis (Austrian triad: pneumonia + meningitis + endocarditis) and purulent pericarditis.','#mikrobiyoloji/endo'],
       ['beyin','Meningitis and delirium','Pneumococcal meningitis can follow bacteraemia. Hypoxia and systemic inflammation may present as delirium in the elderly.',null],
       ['bobrek','Acute kidney injury','Hypoperfusion, sepsis and nephrotoxic drugs. Legionella adds hyponatraemia, diarrhoea and raised liver enzymes.',null],
       ['akciger','ARDS','Severe pneumonia is the commonest cause of ARDS.','#akciger/hastalik/ards']],
 tedavi:['Outpatient, no comorbidity: amoxicillin (or doxycycline/macrolide depending on local resistance)','Admitted: beta-lactam (ceftriaxone) + macrolide, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone alone','Intensive care: beta-lactam + macrolide; broaden for MRSA/Pseudomonas risk','Oxygen (SpO₂ 92–96%), fluids, early antibiotics (within 1 hour in sepsis)']},
{id:'koah',n:'COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis)',tag:'Chronic · progressive',
 ozet:'Progressive airflow limitation that is not fully reversible. In emphysema alveolar walls are destroyed and the lung is over-inflated; in chronic bronchitis mucus hypersecretion in the bronchi dominates.',
 why:['Smoking (the most important; pack-years), biomass and occupational dusts, air pollution','Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (young, non-smoker, lower-lobe panacinar emphysema; liver disease may coexist)','Low birth weight and impaired lung growth in childhood'],
 how:'Smoke recruits macrophages and neutrophils; their elastase and matrix metalloproteinases destroy elastin in the alveolar walls (protease–antiprotease imbalance) and oxidative stress inactivates antiproteases. Elastic recoil is lost and the radial traction that holds small airways open falls: airways close early in expiration and air is trapped (hyperinflation, raised RV and TLC). Centriacinar emphysema affects the upper lobes, panacinar (AAT deficiency) the lower lobes. In chronic bronchitis goblet cell hyperplasia and submucosal gland hypertrophy (Reid index >0.4) produce mucus plugs. Result: V/Q mismatch and hypoxaemia; later hypercapnia, pulmonary hypertension from hypoxic vasoconstriction and cor pulmonale.',
 bul:['Years of exertional dyspnoea that worsens; chronic productive cough (chronic bronchitis: ≥3 months a year for 2 consecutive years)','“Pink puffer” (emphysema-dominant): thin, tachypnoeic, pursed-lip breathing','“Blue bloater” (bronchitis-dominant): cyanosis, oedema, polycythaemia, hypercapnia','Exacerbations: more dyspnoea, more and more purulent sputum (mostly viral/bacterial infection)'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Barrel chest (increased AP diameter), accessory muscles, pursed lips, tripod position; Hoover sign (lower ribs drawn in during inspiration)'],['Palpation','Reduced tactile fremitus; apex beat hard to feel'],['Percussion','Hyperresonant; liver dullness and diaphragm low, reduced diaphragmatic movement'],['Auscultation','Reduced breath sounds, prolonged expiration; wheeze, rhonchi or early inspiratory coarse crackles. Heart sounds distant.']],
 xrt:['Hyperinflation: diaphragm below the 10th–11th posterior rib in the midclavicular line, flattened domes','Increased retrosternal air space (lateral film), a narrow, long “teardrop” heart','Reduced peripheral vascular markings, bullae in the upper lobes','CT: centriacinar (upper lobe) or panacinar (lower lobe) emphysema, bronchial wall thickening'],
 tani:[['Spirometry (required)','A post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC <0.70 confirms fixed airflow limitation. GOLD grade by FEV₁ (% predicted): ≥80 mild, 50–79 moderate, 30–49 severe, <30 very severe.'],
       ['Lung volumes and DLCO','TLC, RV and RV/TLC rise (air trapping). DLCO falls in emphysema (loss of surface); usually normal in chronic bronchitis and asthma.'],
       ['Symptoms and exacerbations','mMRC or CAT score and exacerbations in the last year define the treatment group (GOLD A, B, E). Blood eosinophils ≥300/µL predict ICS response.'],
       ['Arterial blood gas','Chronic hypercapnia: compensated respiratory acidosis (high HCO₃⁻). In an exacerbation, pH <7.35 → non-invasive ventilation.'],
       ['Other','Alpha-1 antitrypsin level (once in every COPD patient), haemoglobin (polycythaemia), ECG/echo (cor pulmonale), CT (emphysema distribution, cancer screening).']],
 etki:[['kalp','Cor pulmonale (right heart failure)','Hypoxic vasoconstriction and loss of the capillary bed raise pulmonary artery pressure; the right ventricle hypertrophies and then fails: raised JVP, hepatomegaly, leg oedema. ECG: right axis, P pulmonale, right bundle branch block.','#durumlar/rbbb'],
       ['kalp','Arrhythmias','Hypoxia, acidosis, beta-agonists and theophylline: multifocal atrial tachycardia and AF become more common.','#durumlar/af'],
       ['kan','Secondary polycythaemia','Chronic hypoxaemia raises renal erythropoietin: haematocrit rises, with higher viscosity and thrombosis risk.',null],
       ['beyin','Hypercapnic encephalopathy','In an exacerbation or with uncontrolled high-flow oxygen PaCO₂ rises: headache, drowsiness, flapping tremor, coma (CO₂ narcosis). Target SpO₂ 88–92%.',null],
       ['sistemik','Cachexia, muscle loss, osteoporosis','Systemic inflammation and inactivity waste skeletal muscle; systemic steroids and vitamin D deficiency add osteoporosis. Depression and anxiety are common.',null],
       ['akciger','Lung cancer and pneumothorax','Smoking raises cancer risk by the same route; a ruptured bulla causes secondary spontaneous pneumothorax.','#akciger/hastalik/ptx']],
 tedavi:['Stopping smoking (the only step that changes the course) and vaccines (influenza, pneumococcus, RSV)','Long-acting bronchodilators: LAMA + LABA; add ICS with frequent exacerbations and eosinophils ≥300','Pulmonary rehabilitation; long-term oxygen ≥15 h/day if resting PaO₂ ≤55 mmHg (prolongs survival)','Exacerbation: SABA ± SAMA, prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days, antibiotics for purulent sputum, NIV for hypercapnic acidosis']},
{id:'astim',n:'Asthma',tag:'Chronic · episodic, reversible',
 ozet:'Chronic airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness with bronchial narrowing that reverses spontaneously or with treatment.',
 why:['Atopy (type 2 immunity), family history, allergens (house dust mite, pollen, animal dander)','Triggers: viral infection, exercise, cold air, tobacco smoke, aspirin and NSAIDs (AERD), beta-blockers','Occupational asthma; a non-type-2 phenotype linked to obesity'],
 how:'Dendritic cells present allergen to Th2 cells. IL-4 and IL-13 drive IgE production by B cells and goblet cell metaplasia; IL-5 drives eosinophil production and survival. In the early phase (minutes) cross-linked IgE on mast cells releases histamine, leukotrienes (LTC₄, D₄) and PGD₂: bronchospasm, oedema and mucus. In the late phase (4–8 hours) eosinophils and Th2 cells sustain the inflammation. Chronically the airway remodels: basement membrane thickening, smooth muscle hypertrophy, subepithelial fibrosis. Narrowing is worse in expiration; air trapping, V/Q mismatch and hypoxaemia follow.',
 bul:['Recurrent wheeze, dyspnoea, chest tightness and cough; worse at night and early morning','Symptoms vary with triggers and over time','Severe attack: unable to complete sentences, sitting forward, SpO₂ <92%, PEF <50%, pulse >120, pulsus paradoxus','Life-threatening: silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, altered consciousness, a normal or rising PaCO₂'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Tachypnoea, accessory muscles, intercostal recession; unable to talk if severe'],['Palpation','Usually normal; reduced chest movement with hyperinflation'],['Percussion','Normal or hyperresonant'],['Auscultation','Polyphonic expiratory wheeze, prolonged expiration. In a very severe attack flow is so low that the wheeze disappears: silent chest (a bad sign).']],
 xrt:['Usually normal; hyperinflation during an attack','The film looks for complications: pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, pneumonia','Segmental collapse from mucus plugs (central bronchiectasis in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis)'],
 tani:[['Spirometry and reversibility','Reduced FEV₁/FVC; an FEV₁ rise of ≥12% and ≥200 mL after salbutamol shows variable airflow limitation. Spirometry between attacks may be normal.'],
       ['Variability','Two weeks of PEF monitoring with mean daily variability >10% (adults); a positive methacholine or mannitol challenge (a negative test largely excludes asthma).'],
       ['Type 2 markers','FeNO >50 ppb, blood eosinophils, total and specific IgE, skin prick tests. Used to choose biologics.'],
       ['During an attack','PEF (% predicted), SpO₂; blood gas if severe: PaCO₂ is low at first from hyperventilation. A normal (40 mmHg) or high PaCO₂ means the patient is tiring: intensive care.'],
       ['Differential diagnosis','Vocal cord dysfunction (flattened inspiratory flow–volume loop), COPD, heart failure (“cardiac asthma”), foreign body, bronchiectasis.']],
 etki:[['akciger','Pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum','Trapped air can rupture alveoli; subcutaneous emphysema in the neck, Hamman crunch.','#akciger/hastalik/ptx'],
       ['kalp','Tachycardia and pulsus paradoxus','In a severe attack the large negative pleural pressure of inspiration drops systolic pressure by >10 mmHg (pulsus paradoxus, as in tamponade). Beta-agonists cause tachycardia and hypokalaemia.','#durumlar/tachy'],
       ['kan','Hypokalaemia','High-dose beta-2 agonists drive potassium into cells; theophylline and steroids add to it.','#durumlar/hypok'],
       ['sistemik','The atopic march and associated disease','Allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps (AERD: asthma + nasal polyps + aspirin sensitivity), atopic dermatitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA).',null]],
 tedavi:['Stepwise treatment (GINA): preferably ICS–formoterol both as maintenance and as needed (MART); SABA-only treatment is not recommended','If uncontrolled: LAMA, LTRA; biologics for severe type 2 asthma (anti-IgE, anti-IL-5/5R, anti-IL-4R, anti-TSLP)','Attack: high-dose SABA + ipratropium, systemic corticosteroid, oxygen (SpO₂ 93–95%), IV magnesium sulphate if severe','Inhaler technique, trigger avoidance, a written action plan']},
{id:'odem',n:'Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema',tag:'Acute · from the left heart',
 ozet:'Raised pulmonary venous pressure in left heart failure pushes fluid first into the interstitium and then into the alveoli.',
 why:['Acute decompensated left heart failure (HFrEF or HFpEF), acute myocardial infarction','Hypertensive emergency, acute mitral or aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis','Tachyarrhythmia (fast AF), fluid overload, renal failure'],
 how:'In the Starling balance hydrostatic pressure overcomes oncotic pressure. When left atrial pressure (PCWP) exceeds ~18 mmHg fluid enters the peribronchovascular interstitium faster than the lymphatics can clear it (Kerley B lines, peribronchial cuffing); above ~25 mmHg it floods the alveoli. Compliance falls (stiff lung: dyspnoea, orthopnoea), alveolar fluid causes shunt and V/Q mismatch, and oedema of small airways produces wheeze (“cardiac asthma”). Sympathetic activation raises afterload and closes a vicious circle.',
 bul:['Sudden severe dyspnoea, orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea','Pink frothy sputum, sweating, agitation, cold peripheries','Tachycardia, hypertension or hypotension (cardiogenic shock), S3 gallop, raised JVP'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Tachypnoea, sitting up, sweating, cyanosis; distended neck veins'],['Palpation','Normal fremitus; the apex beat may be displaced'],['Percussion','Normal; dull at the bases if there is an effusion'],['Auscultation','Bilateral fine crackles starting at the bases and spreading upwards, possibly wheeze; S3 gallop and murmurs over the heart.']],
 xrt:['Cardiomegaly (cardiothoracic ratio >0.5), upper-lobe vascular prominence (upper lobe diversion, PCWP 13–18)','Interstitial oedema: Kerley B lines (short, near-horizontal peripheral lines), peribronchial cuffing, blurred vessel margins (18–25)','Alveolar oedema: perihilar “bat-wing” or “butterfly” opacity (>25), bilateral effusions (right more than left)','Lung ultrasound: diffuse bilateral B-lines (≥3 per zone)'],
 tani:[['Clinical + imaging','Sudden dyspnoea, orthopnoea, crackles and an S3 with a chest film and bedside ultrasound are often enough.'],
       ['Natriuretic peptides','NT-proBNP and BNP are high (in the acute setting NT-proBNP <300 pg/mL largely excludes heart failure). Falsely low in obesity, high in renal failure.'],
       ['Echocardiography','EF, valve disease, LV filling pressure (E/e’), pericardium. Essential to find the cause.'],
       ['ECG and troponin','Look for acute coronary syndrome and arrhythmia (AF) as triggers.'],
       ['Arterial blood gas','Hypoxaemia; low PaCO₂ at first from hyperventilation, lactic and respiratory acidosis when severe.'],
       ['Telling it from ARDS','Cardiogenic: PCWP >18, cardiomegaly, effusions, Kerley B and raised BNP; in ARDS the heart is normal and opacities are peripheral and patchy.']],
 etki:[['kalp','The cause is the heart: left heart failure','Pulmonary oedema is usually a heart problem: HFrEF, HFpEF, acute MI, mitral stenosis and fast AF. See the heart pages.','#durumlar/hfref'],
       ['kalp','Mitral stenosis','Obstructed emptying of the left atrium; oedema starts suddenly with tachycardia and in pregnancy.','#durumlar/ms'],
       ['bobrek','Cardiorenal syndrome','Low output and venous congestion impair renal perfusion: creatinine rises, fluid is retained, oedema worsens.',null],
       ['karaciger','Congestive hepatopathy','Pressure transmitted to the right heart congests the liver: “nutmeg liver” and cholestatic enzyme rise.',null],
       ['plevra','Bilateral transudative effusions','Heart failure is the commonest cause of transudative effusion; it regresses with treatment.','#akciger/hastalik/efuzyon']],
 tedavi:['Sit upright; oxygen if SpO₂ <90%; CPAP/NIV for respiratory distress (reduces preload, opens alveoli)','IV loop diuretic (furosemide): the first drug for congestion','IV nitrate if systolic BP >110 (vasodilator)','Treat the cause: revascularisation in ACS, rate/rhythm control for fast AF; inotropes in cardiogenic shock']},
{id:'pe',n:'Pulmonary embolism',tag:'Acute · vascular obstruction',
 ozet:'A thrombus, usually from the deep veins of the leg, blocks the pulmonary arteries. Ventilation continues but perfusion stops: dead space.',
 why:['Virchow’s triad: stasis (immobilisation, long travel), hypercoagulability (cancer, pregnancy, oestrogen, thrombophilia), endothelial injury (surgery, trauma)','Deep vein thrombosis (especially proximal: popliteal and above)','Rarely fat (long-bone fracture), air or amniotic fluid embolism'],
 how:'The thrombus blocks a pulmonary artery: a ventilated but unperfused area (V/Q = ∞, alveolar dead space). Platelet serotonin and thromboxane cause vasoconstriction and bronchoconstriction. Blood diverted to the remaining lung creates low-V/Q areas → hypoxaemia; hyperventilation → low PaCO₂. The sudden rise in pulmonary vascular resistance dilates the right ventricle: the septum shifts left, LV filling falls, coronary perfusion drops → RV ischaemia, hypotension, obstructive shock. Small distal emboli may cause pulmonary infarction (a pleura-based wedge).',
 bul:['Sudden dyspnoea (commonest), tachypnoea, tachycardia','Pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis (infarction), cough','Syncope, hypotension (massive PE)','Unilateral leg swelling and pain (DVT)'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Tachypnoea, cyanosis; raised JVP (right heart strain)'],['Palpation','Parasternal heave (right ventricle), unilateral calf swelling and tenderness'],['Percussion','Usually normal'],['Auscultation','The lungs are often normal: the mismatch between findings and dyspnoea is the clue. Local crackles and a pleural rub over an infarct. Over the heart a loud P2 and a right-sided S3/S4.']],
 xrt:['The chest film is often normal or non-specific (atelectasis, small effusion, raised hemidiaphragm); its main job is to exclude other diagnoses','Westermark sign: reduced vascularity (oligaemia) beyond the embolus','Hampton hump: a wedge-shaped, pleura-based infarct opacity; Fleischner sign: an enlarged pulmonary artery','CT pulmonary angiography: a filling defect in the artery (the practical gold standard); RV/LV ratio >1 indicates right ventricular strain'],
 tani:[['Clinical probability','Wells or revised Geneva score; at low probability a negative PERC (8 criteria) means no test is needed.'],
       ['D-dimer','Sensitive but not specific: at low/intermediate probability a negative result excludes PE. Over 50, use an age-adjusted cut-off (age × 10 µg/L).'],
       ['CT pulmonary angiography','If PE is likely or the D-dimer is positive. If contrast is contraindicated or in pregnancy, V/Q scintigraphy (normal ventilation, perfusion defect = mismatch).'],
       ['In the unstable patient','If CT is not possible, bedside echocardiography: RV dilatation, McConnell sign, 60/60 sign. Bilateral compression ultrasound for DVT.'],
       ['Risk stratification','sPESI, raised troponin and BNP, RV dysfunction: low, intermediate-low, intermediate-high, high risk (shock).'],
       ['ECG and blood gas','Sinus tachycardia (commonest), right bundle branch block, right axis, T inversion in V1–V4, S1Q3T3. Blood gas: hypoxaemia, hypocapnia, raised A–a gradient.']],
 etki:[['kalp','Acute right ventricular failure and obstructive shock','With a sudden pressure load the right ventricle dilates, the septum is pushed left, the left ventricle cannot fill and output falls. This is how high-risk PE kills. See the heart page for the ECG and 3D view.','#durumlar/pe'],
       ['kalp','Right bundle branch block and tachyarrhythmias','Stretching of the right ventricle can cause new RBBB, sinus tachycardia and AF.','#durumlar/rbbb'],
       ['beyin','Paradoxical embolism and stroke','With a patent foramen ovale (~25% of adults) rising right atrial pressure lets thrombus cross to the left side: ischaemic stroke.',null],
       ['damar','Deep vein thrombosis (the source)','About half of proximal DVTs have silent PE; leg compression ultrasound helps the diagnosis.',null],
       ['kalp','Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension','Unresolved thrombus becomes fibrotic; consider it in ~2–4% with dyspnoea persisting after 3 months (V/Q scan, pulmonary endarterectomy).',null],
       ['plevra','Pleuritic pain and effusion','An infarct next to the pleura causes a friction rub and a small, often blood-stained exudative effusion.','#akciger/hastalik/efuzyon']],
 tedavi:['Anticoagulation: a DOAC (apixaban, rivaroxaban) or LMWH for most; start before results if suspicion is high','High risk (shock): systemic thrombolysis (alteplase); surgical embolectomy or catheter-directed therapy if contraindicated','Duration: 3 months for a transient risk factor; extended if unprovoked or the risk persists','LMWH in cancer and pregnancy; an IVC filter if anticoagulation is impossible']},
{id:'ptx',n:'Pneumothorax (and tension pneumothorax)',tag:'Acute · air in the pleural space',
 ozet:'Air enters the pleural space, the negative pressure is lost and the lung recoils towards the hilum by its own elasticity. Example: the right lung.',
 why:['Primary spontaneous: tall, thin young men, smoking; rupture of an apical subpleural bleb','Secondary spontaneous: underlying lung disease (COPD/bullae, cystic fibrosis, PJP, TB, LAM)','Traumatic: rib fracture, penetrating injury; iatrogenic: central line, pleural aspiration, mechanical ventilation (barotrauma)'],
 how:'When pleural pressure equals atmospheric pressure the negative pressure binding lung to chest wall is lost: the lung falls in, the chest wall springs out. Unventilated but perfused areas of the collapsed lung cause shunt and hypoxaemia. In tension pneumothorax the tear acts as a one-way valve: air enters with each breath but cannot leave, pleural pressure becomes positive, the mediastinum is pushed to the other side, the venae cavae kink, venous return and output fall → obstructive shock. It is a clinical diagnosis; do not wait for imaging.',
 bul:['Sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnoea','Tachycardia; a small primary pneumothorax may give few signs','Tension: severe distress, hypotension, tachycardia, raised JVP, trachea deviated away, hypoxaemia; in a ventilated patient a sudden rise in airway pressure'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Reduced movement on the affected side, which may look larger; in tension raised JVP, trachea deviated away'],['Palpation','Reduced or absent fremitus; crepitus with subcutaneous emphysema (like walking on snow)'],['Percussion','Hyperresonant (tympanic)'],['Auscultation','Reduced or absent breath sounds; reduced vocal resonance.']],
 xrt:['Visceral pleural line: a thin white line with black space beyond it and no lung markings','Upright, air collects at the apex; supine (intensive care) it collects anteriorly: deep sulcus sign','Tension: mediastinal shift away, flattened/inverted diaphragm, widened intercostal spaces','Lung ultrasound: no lung sliding and no B-lines, “barcode” sign in M-mode, a lung point is diagnostic'],
 tani:[['Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis','Shock + unilateral loss of breath sounds + tracheal deviation: needle decompression without waiting for a film (2nd intercostal space midclavicular line or 4th–5th space anterior-mid axillary line) followed by a chest drain.'],
       ['Film','PA chest film; size by the distance between chest wall and lung edge at the hilum (BTS: ≥2 cm large) or apex-to-cupola distance (ACCP: ≥3 cm).'],
       ['Ultrasound and CT','In trauma and intensive care, ultrasound is more sensitive than the film. CT shows small pneumothoraces, bullae and underlying disease.'],
       ['Arterial blood gas','Hypoxaemia (shunt), usually hyperventilation; hypercapnia from the underlying disease in secondary pneumothorax.']],
 etki:[['kalp','Obstructive shock (tension)','Positive pleural pressure cuts venous return: tachycardia, hypotension, raised JVP; untreated, arrest with pulseless electrical activity (PEA). The same physiology as tamponade.','#durumlar/tamponade'],
       ['akciger','Re-expansion oedema','Rapid re-expansion of a lung collapsed for a long time (>72 h) can cause unilateral pulmonary oedema.','#akciger/hastalik/odem'],
       ['sistemik','Subcutaneous emphysema and pneumomediastinum','Air spreads into soft tissue: swelling and crepitus in the neck and face.',null]],
 tedavi:['Tension: immediate needle decompression and a chest drain','Primary, small and asymptomatic: oxygen and observation (oxygen speeds absorption of pleural air four-fold)','Large or symptomatic: needle aspiration or a small-bore chest drain; low threshold for a drain and admission in secondary pneumothorax','Recurrent: VATS bullectomy and pleurodesis; restrictions on flying and diving']},
{id:'efuzyon',n:'Pleural effusion',tag:'Fluid in the pleural space',
 ozet:'More fluid than normal accumulates in the pleural space. Example: a moderate left effusion with a meniscus.',
 why:['Transudate (systemic imbalance): heart failure (commonest), cirrhosis (hepatic hydrothorax), nephrotic syndrome, hypoalbuminaemia','Exudate (the pleura itself is diseased): parapneumonic/empyema, malignancy (lung, breast, lymphoma), tuberculosis, pulmonary embolism, pancreatitis, autoimmune (RA, SLE)','Haemothorax (trauma), chylothorax (thoracic duct injury)'],
 how:'Fluid filters from systemic capillaries of the parietal pleura and drains through lymphatic stomata. In a transudate hydrostatic pressure rises or oncotic pressure falls (pleura intact, low protein). In an exudate inflammation or tumour increases capillary permeability and/or blocks lymphatic drainage (high protein and LDH). The fluid compresses the underlying lung (passive/compressive atelectasis) and adds a restrictive load; a large effusion inverts the diaphragm and pushes the mediastinum away.',
 bul:['Exertional dyspnoea (depending on size), dry cough','Pleuritic pain (exudate, inflammation), fever (empyema), weight loss (malignancy, TB)','A small effusion may give no signs'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Reduced movement on the affected side; full intercostal spaces with a large effusion'],['Palpation','Reduced or absent tactile fremitus'],['Percussion','“Stony” dullness; the upper border is higher laterally (Damoiseau line). Dullness develops first at the costophrenic angle.'],['Auscultation','Reduced or absent breath sounds; bronchial breathing and egophony at the upper border of the fluid, over the compressed lung.']],
 xrt:['Blunting of the costophrenic angle (~200 mL on a PA film; ~50 mL in the posterior recess on a lateral film)','Meniscus sign: a concave upper border curving upwards laterally','A large effusion opacifies the hemithorax and shifts the mediastinum away (if it does not shift: collapse from a blocked bronchus or a fixed mediastinum; think malignancy)','Ultrasound: anechoic or septated fluid; guides the tap. On a lateral decubitus film free fluid moves.'],
 tani:[['Thoracentesis','Diagnostic tap for an effusion of unknown cause (≥1 cm on lateral decubitus). A bilateral, symmetrical effusion in the setting of heart failure can first be treated with diuretics and watched.'],
       ['Light’s criteria','Pleural/serum protein >0.5, pleural/serum LDH >0.6 or pleural LDH > two-thirds of the serum upper limit: any one = exudate. Heart failure on diuretics may look like an exudate: a serum–pleural albumin gradient >1.2 g/dL indicates a transudate.'],
       ['Other fluid tests','Appearance (pus, blood, milk), pH and glucose (pH <7.2 and glucose <60: complicated parapneumonic/empyema → drain), cell count (lymphocytic: TB, malignancy), Gram stain and culture, cytology, ADA (>40 U/L: TB), triglycerides (>110 mg/dL: chylothorax), amylase (pancreatitis, oesophageal rupture).'],
       ['Further tests','If cytology is negative and suspicion persists: contrast CT of the chest, pleural biopsy or thoracoscopy.']],
 etki:[['kalp','Heart failure (the commonest cause)','The commonest cause of bilateral transudative effusion; it may be larger on the right. Regresses with diuretics.','#durumlar/hfref'],
       ['kalp','Pericarditis and after cardiac surgery','Pericarditis and Dressler (post-pericardiotomy) syndrome may come with a left effusion.','#durumlar/peri'],
       ['karaciger','Cirrhosis: hepatic hydrothorax','Ascitic fluid passes through diaphragmatic defects; mostly right-sided, transudate.',null],
       ['bobrek','Nephrotic syndrome and renal failure','Low oncotic pressure (albumin loss) and hypervolaemia cause transudates; uraemia can cause fibrinous pleuritis.',null],
       ['sistemik','Malignancy and systemic disease','Lung and breast cancer, lymphoma, mesothelioma; RA (very low glucose), SLE, pancreatitis (left, high amylase). A malignant effusion means stage IV (M1a).','#akciger/hastalik/kanser']],
 tedavi:['Treat the cause (diuretics for a transudate)','Therapeutic thoracentesis for a large symptomatic effusion (≤1.5 L at a time: re-expansion oedema)','Complicated parapneumonic/empyema: chest drain + antibiotics ± intrapleural tPA/DNase','Recurrent malignant effusion: talc pleurodesis or a tunnelled pleural catheter']},
{id:'tb',n:'Pulmonary tuberculosis (reactivation)',tag:'Chronic · granulomatous',
 ozet:'A chronic infection by Mycobacterium tuberculosis with caseating granulomas. Reactivation TB forms cavities in the apical-posterior segments of the upper lobes.',
 why:['Organism: M. tuberculosis (acid-fast, aerobic, slow-growing bacillus); spread through the air in droplet nuclei','Risk: HIV (the strongest), diabetes, silicosis, TNF-α inhibitors and corticosteroids, chronic kidney disease, malnutrition, crowding, endemic areas','Primary infection usually becomes latent; lifetime reactivation risk ~5–10%'],
 how:'The bacillus multiplies in the alveolar macrophage by blocking phagolysosome fusion. After 3–8 weeks cell-mediated immunity develops: Th1 cells activate macrophages with IFN-γ and TNF-α maintains the granuloma. The granuloma has central caseous necrosis surrounded by epithelioid cells, Langhans giant cells and lymphocytes. In primary infection a lower-mid zone Ghon focus + hilar node = Ghon complex (Ranke complex when calcified). When immunity weakens the bacilli multiply again in the apical segments where oxygen is highest; when caseous material drains into a bronchus a cavity forms (the most infectious period). Bronchial spread gives a tree-in-bud pattern, haematogenous spread miliary TB.',
 bul:['Cough for weeks (>2–3 weeks), sputum, haemoptysis','Evening fever, night sweats, weight loss, anorexia, fatigue','Pleuritic pain (TB pleurisy); atypical and disseminated disease in HIV'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Cachexia; flattening of the upper chest in advanced disease'],['Palpation','Increased fremitus over consolidation'],['Percussion','Dull over the upper zones'],['Auscultation','Apical post-tussive crackles (on deep inspiration after a cough), bronchial breathing; amphoric breathing over a large cavity.']],
 xrt:['Infiltrate and a thick-walled cavity in the apical-posterior segment of the upper lobe (or the superior segment of the lower lobe)','Tree-in-bud pattern: endobronchial spread','Primary TB: Ghon focus and hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy; miliary TB: diffuse 1–3 mm nodules in both lungs','Healing: fibrosis, volume loss, calcification, hilar elevation'],
 tani:[['Sputum','At least 2–3 sputum samples for acid-fast staining (Ziehl–Neelsen) and mycobacterial culture (gold standard; 2–6 weeks). Induced sputum or bronchoscopic lavage if the patient cannot produce sputum.'],
       ['Molecular test','Xpert MTB/RIF (NAAT): TB DNA and rifampicin resistance (rpoB) within hours. Recommended as the first test.'],
       ['Tests for latent infection','Tuberculin skin test (PPD; may be falsely positive after BCG) and IGRA (QuantiFERON; unaffected by BCG). They do not distinguish active disease.'],
       ['Imaging','Film and CT (cavity, tree-in-bud, lymphadenopathy).'],
       ['Extrapulmonary','Lymphocytic exudate with raised ADA in pleural fluid; caseating granuloma on pleural biopsy. Test every TB patient for HIV.'],
       ['Infection control','Airborne isolation in a negative-pressure room with N95 masks when pulmonary TB is suspected; contact tracing.']],
 etki:[['kalp','Tuberculous pericarditis and constrictive pericarditis','The commonest cause of large pericardial effusion in endemic areas; the thickened pericardium may constrict as it heals.','#durumlar/peri'],
       ['endokrin','Adrenal insufficiency (Addison)','Involvement of both adrenals causes cortisol deficiency: hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperpigmentation.',null],
       ['kemik','Pott disease (spinal TB)','Thoracolumbar vertebral and disc involvement, cold abscess, gibbus deformity, neurological deficit.',null],
       ['beyin','TB meningitis and tuberculoma','Basal meningitis: cranial nerve palsies, hydrocephalus; CSF with lymphocytes, low glucose, very high protein.',null],
       ['bobrek','Genitourinary TB','Sterile pyuria (culture-negative leucocyturia), haematuria, ureteric stricture.',null],
       ['plevra','TB pleurisy','A delayed hypersensitivity reaction: lymphocytic exudate, raised ADA.','#akciger/hastalik/efuzyon'],
       ['karaciger','Drug hepatotoxicity','Isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide can raise liver enzymes; monitor. Isoniazid causes neuropathy through B6 deficiency.',null]],
 tedavi:['Drug-sensitive TB: 2 months of isoniazid + rifampicin + pyrazinamide + ethambutol (HRZE), then 4 months of HR (6 months in total); directly observed therapy','Pyridoxine (B6) with isoniazid; rifampicin is an enzyme inducer (oral contraceptive and antiretroviral interactions)','Latent TB: 3 months of rifapentine + isoniazid, 4 months of rifampicin or 6–9 months of isoniazid','Multidrug-resistant TB: bedaquiline-containing regimens (BPaLM)']},
{id:'ipf',n:'Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF)',tag:'Chronic · restrictive',
 ozet:'Progressive scarring of the lung of unknown cause in older adults. Basal and subpleural honeycombing (UIP pattern).',
 why:['Repeated alveolar epithelial injury: smoking, occupational dust, gastro-oesophageal reflux, viral infections','Genetic predisposition (MUC5B promoter variant, telomere genes), age >60, male sex','Exclude: asbestosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, connective tissue disease, drugs (amiodarone, methotrexate, bleomycin, nitrofurantoin)'],
 how:'Repeated injury and failed repair of type II pneumocytes release fibrogenic signals, above all TGF-β. Fibroblasts become myofibroblasts and lay down excess collagen in fibroblastic foci; alveoli are destroyed and turn into cystic honeycomb spaces. The lung stiffens: compliance falls, all lung volumes shrink (restrictive pattern), and because elastic recoil increases FEV₁/FVC is normal or high. The thickened blood–air barrier impairs diffusion (low DLCO): marked desaturation on exercise. Chronic hypoxia leads to pulmonary hypertension.',
 bul:['Insidious, progressive exertional dyspnoea and dry cough','Finger clubbing (25–50%)','Acute exacerbation: hypoxaemia worsening over weeks'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Tachypnoea, shallow breathing; clubbing; cyanosis in late disease'],['Palpation','Reduced chest expansion'],['Percussion','Normal'],['Auscultation','Bilateral basal, late inspiratory, dry “Velcro” fine crackles.']],
 xrt:['Film: basal and peripheral reticular opacities, reduced lung volumes','HRCT: basal and subpleural reticulation, traction bronchiectasis and honeycombing (UIP pattern)','Ground glass is not dominant (if it is, think of another diagnosis)','Rapid decline: new bilateral ground glass in an acute exacerbation'],
 tani:[['HRCT','A typical UIP pattern + a compatible clinical picture + exclusion of other causes is enough; most patients do not need a biopsy.'],
       ['Lung function','Restrictive pattern: reduced TLC and FVC; FEV₁/FVC normal or high. DLCO markedly reduced. An FVC fall ≥10% a year carries a poor prognosis.'],
       ['6-minute walk test','Exercise desaturation (SpO₂ <88%) and distance; prognosis and oxygen need.'],
       ['Laboratory','ANA, RF, anti-CCP and myositis antibodies for connective tissue disease; exposure history and, if needed, BAL (lymphocytosis) for hypersensitivity pneumonitis.'],
       ['Multidisciplinary discussion','Respiratory medicine, radiology and pathology decide together; cryobiopsy or surgical biopsy if uncertain.']],
 etki:[['kalp','Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale','Chronic hypoxia and loss of the capillary bed strain the right ventricle; this worsens the prognosis.','#durumlar/rbbb'],
       ['sistemik','Clubbing and hypertrophic osteoarthropathy','Soft-tissue enlargement of the fingertips; megakaryocyte-derived PDGF and VEGF are implicated.',null],
       ['akciger','Lung cancer','The risk of lung cancer rises in IPF; it develops in fibrotic areas.','#akciger/hastalik/kanser'],
       ['sistemik','Gastro-oesophageal reflux and sleep apnoea','Commonly associated; microaspiration is thought to drive progression.',null]],
 tedavi:['Antifibrotics: nintedanib (tyrosine kinase inhibitor) or pirfenidone; slow FVC decline by ~50%','Oxygen (rest or exercise hypoxaemia), pulmonary rehabilitation, vaccines','Corticosteroid and immunosuppressant combinations are harmful in IPF (outside exacerbations)','Lung transplant in suitable patients; stop smoking']},
{id:'kanser',n:'Lung cancer',tag:'Malignancy · central and peripheral',
 ozet:'The leading cause of cancer death. Example: a central mass at the right hilum (squamous or small cell) and a peripheral nodule in the left upper lobe (adenocarcinoma).',
 why:['Smoking (~85% of cases; risk rises with pack-years), passive smoking','Radon (the commonest cause in never-smokers), asbestos (multiplicative with smoking), arsenic, chromium, nickel, air pollution','Fibrosis, COPD, family history; EGFR/ALK driver-mutated adenocarcinoma in young non-smoking women'],
 how:'Mutations accumulated from carcinogens (TP53, KRAS, EGFR, ALK, ROS1…) cause uncontrolled growth. Non-small cell (NSCLC, ~85%): adenocarcinoma (commonest, peripheral, glandular, TTF-1+), squamous cell (central, keratin pearls, p40+, cavitation, hypercalcaemia), large cell. Small cell (SCLC, ~15%): central, neuroendocrine, grows very fast, spreads early, paraneoplastic syndromes are common. Central tumours block the bronchus and cause collapse and post-obstructive pneumonia; peripheral tumours invade pleura and chest wall. Spread: hilar and mediastinal nodes, brain, bone, liver, adrenal.',
 bul:['A new or changing cough, haemoptysis, dyspnoea, chest pain','Weight loss, fatigue, recurrent pneumonia (in the same lobe)','Local spread: hoarseness (recurrent nerve), Horner syndrome and arm pain (Pancoast), superior vena cava syndrome, diaphragmatic palsy (phrenic), dysphagia','Paraneoplastic: hypercalcaemia, hyponatraemia, Cushing, myasthenia-like weakness, clubbing'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Cachexia, clubbing; in SVC syndrome facial and neck swelling and chest wall venous collaterals; Horner (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)'],['Palpation','Supraclavicular lymph node; reduced fremitus with collapse or effusion'],['Percussion','Dull over collapse or effusion'],['Auscultation','Local monophonic wheeze with partial bronchial obstruction; absent breath sounds with complete obstruction.']],
 xrt:['Central: hilar mass, hilar enlargement; post-obstructive lobar collapse (Golden S sign in the right upper lobe)','Peripheral: a spiculated solitary nodule or mass, pleural tag; cavitation with thick walls in squamous cell','Mediastinal widening (nodes), pleural effusion, rib destruction','CT: staging (TNM); PET-CT: nodes and distant metastases; brain MRI'],
 tani:[['Imaging','Contrast CT of the chest and upper abdomen; nodules followed by the Fleischner rules. Low-dose CT screening reduces mortality in people aged 50–80 with ≥20 pack-years.'],
       ['Tissue diagnosis','Choose the least invasive method that also stages: bronchoscopy and EBUS-TBNA (nodes) for central tumours, CT-guided transthoracic biopsy for peripheral lesions, cytology for an effusion.'],
       ['Molecular testing','In NSCLC (especially adenocarcinoma): EGFR, ALK, ROS1, KRAS G12C, BRAF, MET, RET, NTRK and PD-L1 to choose targeted therapy and immunotherapy.'],
       ['Staging','PET-CT, brain MRI; TNM. SCLC is classified as limited (one radiotherapy field) or extensive stage.'],
       ['Fitness','For surgical candidates FEV₁ and DLCO (predicted postoperative values), and cardiopulmonary exercise testing if needed.'],
       ['Paraneoplastic screen','Calcium (PTHrP), sodium (SIADH), cortisol (ectopic ACTH).']],
 etki:[['beyin','Brain metastases and neurological paraneoplastic syndromes','Headache, seizures, focal deficit. In SCLC: Lambert–Eaton (antibodies to presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels: proximal weakness that improves with repetition), anti-Hu encephalomyelitis, cerebellar degeneration.',null],
       ['endokrin','Hyponatraemia (SIADH) and Cushing','SCLC can secrete ADH and ACTH: euvolaemic hyponatraemia, hypokalaemic alkalosis, hyperglycaemia.',null],
       ['kemik','Hypercalcaemia and bone metastases','Squamous cell cancer secretes PTHrP: confusion, polyuria, constipation, short QT. Bone metastases cause pain and pathological fractures.',null],
       ['damar','Superior vena cava syndrome','A right hilar/mediastinal mass compresses the SVC: swelling of face and arms, dyspnoea, headache; worse on bending forward.',null],
       ['kalp','Pericardial effusion and tamponade','Pericardial metastases can cause a malignant effusion and tamponade; new AF can also be a clue to involvement.','#durumlar/tamponade'],
       ['endokrin','Adrenal and liver metastases','Adrenal metastases are common but usually silent; liver involvement causes raised enzymes and jaundice.',null],
       ['plevra','Malignant pleural effusion','Means stage IV disease (M1a); it recurs and needs pleurodesis or a catheter.','#akciger/hastalik/efuzyon'],
       ['sistemik','Venous thromboembolism (Trousseau)','Adenocarcinoma causes hypercoagulability: higher risk of DVT and pulmonary embolism.','#akciger/hastalik/pe']],
 tedavi:['Early NSCLC: surgery (lobectomy) or stereotactic radiotherapy if unfit for surgery; ± adjuvant/neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy','Locally advanced: chemoradiotherapy followed by durvalumab','Advanced: a targeted drug if a driver mutation is present (e.g. osimertinib for EGFR, alectinib for ALK); otherwise immunotherapy ± chemotherapy guided by PD-L1','SCLC: platinum + etoposide + immunotherapy; concurrent radiotherapy in limited stage; stopping smoking at every stage']},
{id:'ards',n:'ARDS (acute respiratory distress syndrome)',tag:'Acute · non-cardiogenic oedema',
 ozet:'Diffuse alveolar damage after a direct or indirect injury: permeability (non-cardiogenic) pulmonary oedema with refractory hypoxaemia.',
 why:['Direct (pulmonary): pneumonia (commonest), aspiration, inhalation injury, near-drowning, lung contusion','Indirect (extrapulmonary): sepsis (commonest indirect), major trauma, acute pancreatitis, multiple transfusions (TRALI), burns','Drugs and toxins'],
 how:'The injury damages alveolar epithelium (type I and II) and capillary endothelium; neutrophils and cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-α) increase permeability. Protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli; dead cells and fibrin form hyaline membranes (exudative phase, week 1). Loss of surfactant collapses alveoli, especially in dependent (dorsal) regions: the “baby lung” (the aerated lung is very small). Shunt and V/Q mismatch cause oxygen-refractory hypoxaemia and compliance falls. In the proliferative phase (weeks 1–3) type II cells multiply; some patients progress to a fibrotic phase.',
 bul:['Rapidly worsening dyspnoea and tachypnoea within 1 week of the risk factor','Hypoxaemia that does not improve with high-flow oxygen','Bilateral widespread crackles; signs of the triggering illness (sepsis, shock)'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Severe tachypnoea, accessory muscles, cyanosis'],['Palpation','Non-specific'],['Percussion','May be dull over dorsal regions'],['Auscultation','Bilateral widespread fine crackles; possibly bronchial breathing.']],
 xrt:['Bilateral, widespread, often peripheral and patchy alveolar opacities; few effusions or Kerley B lines','Normal heart size (unlike cardiogenic oedema)','CT: dense consolidation in dorsal (dependent) regions, normal or ground glass anteriorly'],
 tani:[['Berlin definition','Onset within 1 week; bilateral opacities (not explained by effusion, collapse or nodules); respiratory failure not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload; PaO₂/FiO₂ on PEEP/CPAP ≥5 cmH₂O.'],
       ['Severity (PaO₂/FiO₂)','Mild 200–300, moderate 100–200, severe ≤100 mmHg. The new global definition also covers SpO₂/FiO₂ and patients on high-flow oxygen.'],
       ['Exclude cardiac oedema','Echocardiography (normal LV), BNP; PCWP <18 mmHg if needed.'],
       ['Find the cause','Cultures, lactate, lipase, imaging; drug and transfusion history.']],
 etki:[['kan','Multi-organ failure','ARDS patients usually die not of hypoxaemia but of the underlying sepsis and multi-organ failure.',null],
       ['kalp','Acute cor pulmonale','Hypoxic vasoconstriction, microthrombi and high ventilator pressures strain the right ventricle (~20%); prone positioning and low plateau pressures protect the right heart.','#durumlar/pe'],
       ['bobrek','Acute kidney injury','Sepsis, hypotension and positive-pressure ventilation impair renal perfusion.',null],
       ['beyin','Long-term cognitive and muscle problems','Post-intensive care syndrome: muscle weakness, cognitive impairment, depression, post-traumatic stress.',null]],
 tedavi:['Lung-protective ventilation: tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg ideal body weight, plateau pressure <30 cmH₂O, adequate PEEP','Prone positioning 12–16 h/day in moderate-severe ARDS; neuromuscular blockade early in selected patients','Conservative fluid strategy; treat the cause (antibiotics, source control)','VV-ECMO for refractory hypoxaemia; corticosteroids in severe community-acquired pneumonia and COVID-19']},
{id:'atelektazi',n:'Atelectasis (obstructive lobar collapse)',tag:'Volume loss',
 ozet:'Part of the lung loses its air and collapses. Example: right upper-lobe collapse from a blocked bronchus.',
 why:['Obstructive (resorptive): mucus plug (post-operative, asthma), tumour, foreign body, malpositioned endotracheal tube','Compressive: pleural effusion, pneumothorax, large mass; passive: diaphragmatic immobility','Adhesive: surfactant loss (neonatal RDS, ARDS); cicatricial: fibrosis (TB)'],
 how:'When a bronchus is blocked the trapped air beyond it is absorbed into the blood (O₂ first, then slowly N₂; faster with high FiO₂) and the alveoli collapse. The lobe shrinks: fissures, hilum, trachea and mediastinum are pulled towards the lesion, the diaphragm rises and neighbouring lobes over-inflate in compensation. The perfused but unventilated area is a shunt, although hypoxic vasoconstriction limits it. With persistent obstruction post-obstructive pneumonia develops distally.',
 bul:['Small areas are silent; large ones cause dyspnoea, tachycardia, hypoxaemia','Fever on post-operative days 1–2 (traditionally attributed to atelectasis, weak evidence)','Signs of the underlying cause (haemoptysis, weight loss, a history of foreign body aspiration)'],
 mua:[['Inspection','Reduced movement on the affected side'],['Palpation','Trachea pulled towards the affected side; reduced fremitus'],['Percussion','Dull'],['Auscultation','Reduced or absent breath sounds (bronchial breathing if the bronchus is open).']],
 xrt:['Direct signs: displaced fissure, crowding of vessels and bronchi, increased density of the lobe','Indirect signs: hilar displacement (upwards in right upper-lobe collapse), trachea and mediastinum pulled to the same side, raised diaphragm, narrowed intercostal spaces','Right upper lobe: the horizontal fissure swings up and medially; with a hilar mass it forms a reverse S (Golden S sign)','Left upper lobe: a “veil”-like opacity and the Luftsichel sign'],
 tani:[['Film and CT','CT clarifies the site and cause of collapse (mass, foreign body, mucus).'],
       ['Bronchoscopy','In adults with unexplained lobar collapse, to exclude tumour and foreign body; therapeutic for mucus plugs.'],
       ['Differential diagnosis','Unlike pneumonia: volume loss (structures pulled towards the lesion). Unlike effusion: the mediastinum moves towards the lesion, not away.']],
 etki:[['akciger','Post-obstructive pneumonia','Secretions trapped beyond the obstruction become infected; recurrent pneumonia in the same lobe should raise suspicion of a tumour.','#akciger/hastalik/pnomoni'],
       ['akciger','Underlying cause: lung cancer','In adults a central tumour is an important cause of lobar collapse.','#akciger/hastalik/kanser'],
       ['kalp','Tachycardia and hypoxaemia','A sudden large collapse (e.g. a whole lung) can cause severe hypoxaemia and tachycardia.','#durumlar/tachy']],
 tedavi:['Post-operative prevention: early mobilisation, incentive spirometry, pain control, deep-breathing exercises','Mucus plug: chest physiotherapy, suction, bronchoscopy if needed','Foreign body and tumour: treat the cause (bronchoscopic removal, surgery, stent)']}
];

window.KalpI18n&&window.KalpI18n.add({ORGANS:{akciger:{n:'Lungs','LENSES~':LENSES,'PARTS~':PARTS,'HISTO~':HISTO,'EMBRYO~':EMBRYO,'SESLER~':SESLER,MUA_TABLO,CURB65,WELLS,LIGHT,GRP,'HAST~':HAST}},
 UI:{'Kalp ve akciğer · aktif':'Heart and lungs · active','Akciğer atlası':'Lung atlas'}});
